  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>It is an example of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;reaction.</p><p>Rate of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>reaction</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>stability</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>carbocation</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math></p><p>In option 1 carbocation&nbsp;formed after removing halogen is destabilized by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;effect.</p><p>In option 2 carbocation&nbsp;formed after removing halogen is stabilized by +M effect since lone pair is present at oxygen atom.</p><p>In option 3&nbsp;carbocation&nbsp;formed after removing halogen is stabilized by +M effect of lone pair present on nitrogen atom but lone pair is also in conjugation with benzene ring therefore +M effect is less.</p><p>In option 4&nbsp;carbocation&nbsp;formed after removing halogen is stabilized by +M effect of lone pair present on nitrogen atom and +M effect of nitrogen atom is more than that of oxygen atom. Hence, option 4 is most stable.&nbsp;</p>",
    "image": null
  },
